\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Positive quadratic actions}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{(E,h)} be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space,
with #{n=\dim E\geq0}, its finite-dimensional topology, Borel sigma-algebra
and [canonical Euclidean volume](fdip-002M). The field specialization
is #{E=X_V=\operatorname{Map}(V,\mathbb R)} for a finite vertex set,
including the empty set, with counting inner product
#{h_V(x,y)=\sum_{v\in V}x(v)y(v)} and
[coordinate Lebesgue measure](fdip-002H). There are finitely many
coordinates but, in positive dimension, continuously many configurations.
Real-spin integration uses this reference convention in
\citet{Definition 8.1 and Equation (8.3), author version p. 381}{friedli2017statistical}.
The extension to #{(E,h)} is the same paired construction in an
orthonormal chart; it asserts no ring-general probability law.}

\p{A \newvocab{positive precision parameter} is a real linear
endomorphism #{Q:E\to E} that is [self-adjoint](fdip-001P)
for #{h} and
strictly positive: #{h(x,Qx)>0} for every #{x\ne0}.
Define its symmetric bilinear form and associated quadratic form by
##{b_Q(x,y)=h(x,Qy),\qquad q_Q(x)=b_Q(x,x).}
Linearity in both slots follows from linearity of #{Q} and the pairing.
Self-adjointness gives
#{b_Q(y,x)=h(Qy,x)=h(y,Qx)=b_Q(x,y)}.
Consequently
##{q_Q(x+y)-q_Q(x)-q_Q(y)=2b_Q(x,y).}
Also #{q_Q(cx)=c^2q_Q(x)} for every real #{c}. This is the
\vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004} used here; positivity of its diagonal
alone would not assert self-adjointness of an arbitrary endomorphism.}

\p{Its \newvocab{Euclidean quadratic action} and
\newvocab{Boltzmann weight} are
##{\begin{aligned}
S_Q(x)&=\frac12 q_Q(x)=\frac12h(x,Qx),\\
w_Q(x)&=\exp(-S_Q(x)).
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{q_Q=2S_Q}, #{S_Q(0)=0}, and #{S_Q(x)>0} off zero.
Dividing the polarization identity by two gives
##{S_Q(x+y)-S_Q(x)-S_Q(y)=b_Q(x,y).}
The finite-coordinate formulas make #{S_Q} continuous, hence
[Borel measurable](fdip-002G), and so is its exponential.
In particular #{0<w_Q(x)\le1}, with equality #{w_Q(x)=1} exactly
when #{x=0}. This bound does not prove integrability
on a space of infinite reference volume; [Gaussian normalization](fdip-002T)
supplies that separate argument.}

\p{The [counting-array bridge](fdip-002O) recovers the familiar
symmetric matrix and #{x^TQx} notation from this operator definition.
The variables and coefficients here are dimensionless: their chosen
scales are absorbed into #{Q}. Wingate's Euclidean convention is
#{\exp(-S)}; his lattice-spacing factors belong to his lattice model
\citet{Sections 3-4, Equations (3.1) and (4.1)-(4.6), pp. 2-3}{wingate2025lattice}.
This real nonnegative weight supplies no real-time evolution.
The [complete four-cycle action](fdip-002P) gives the concrete operator,
array, half factor and test-field value.}
